WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:08.460
The Wall Street Journal recently
reported on the so-called Blade Runner

00:00:08.469 --> 00:00:14.730
curse. In that 1982 sci-fi cult classic
starring Harrison Ford, some real-world

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:19.210
corporations of 1982 appear prominently
in the dark neon-lit

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:24.100
cityscape of a fictional 2019. Director
Ridley Scott's goal was to convey a

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:28.480
gloomy foreboding about the triumph of
corporate power in our not-too-distant

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:31.360
future.
The curse is that many of the companies

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.890
in the movie, such as Pan Am and Bell
Telephone, no longer exist or were

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:40.809
gobbled up. It's a curse allegedly
because this is supposedly unusual. But

00:00:40.809 --> 00:00:44.530
for all of their corporate power, big
corporations are often powerless when it

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:49.749
comes to the simple task of surviving!
Only 67 of the firms in the Fortune 500

00:00:49.749 --> 00:00:56.409
in 1955 were still there by 2011. The
rest were lost, like tears in rain. The

00:00:56.409 --> 00:00:59.469
lesson is that if you're worried about
corporate power, don't be afraid of the

00:00:59.469 --> 00:01:04.180
market, but do be afraid of companies
joining with governments to artificially

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:06.200
extend their lifespans. What do you think
about corporate power and our dystopian

